WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:04.720
Iron Man is the greatest of all the heroes
of the Marvel Cinematic Universe — so why

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.940
did his solo films stop at three?

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:11.740
In a recent Vanity Fair interview, Avengers:
Endgame screenwriters Christopher Markus and

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.240
Stephen McFeely shed some light on why the
Armored Avenger never got a fourth solo outing.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:21.080
Simply put, the scribes related that Marvel
Studios brass felt that another flick centered

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:25.380
on the adventures of the MCU's most popular
character would've been a little bit too safe.

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:30.140
McFeely made the point that Marvel has never
been particularly interested in doing exactly

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:31.220
what's expected of it.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.640
For evidence, he pointed to its decision to
take a chance on one property in particular

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.840
— James Gunn's Guardians of the Galaxy — rather
than cranking out sequels for its most popular

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.700
Avengers one after another.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.020
"I recognize this animal.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.800
We'd roast them over a flame pit as children.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.240
Their flesh is quite delicious."

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.040
"Not helping!"

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.420
McFeely explained:

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:54.820
"Whether you like all of the 24 movies or
not, the capital that Marvel built up allowed

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:58.700
them to do things like make a movie starring
a raccoon and a tree, right?

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:02.030
You would've already had Iron Man 4 if it
was any other studio.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.780
But they decided, 'No, we're going to take
chances on all these other things.'"

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.320
To illustrate further how Marvel's affinity
for risk-taking has paid off, the scribes

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:14.290
suggested that we should look no further than
Endgame — the highest-grossing film of all

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:20.310
time, and one that definitively removed Iron
Man and Captain America, the MCU's most recognizable

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:22.620
players, from its increasingly crowded board.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.030
McFeely admitted, quote, "To put a flag in
the ground and say, 'We're going to end something

00:01:27.030 --> 00:01:31.979
and take characters off the table' is, I think,
kind of daring, but selfishly it was really

00:01:31.979 --> 00:01:33.060
great for us."

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.590
Markus then chimed in with the final word
not only on why Iron Man 4 never happened

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:42.180
and never will, but why Tony Stark had to
meet his end at the conclusion of Endgame.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:43.120
He said:

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.640
"[A story] needs an end or it loses meaning.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.320
The end is what cements the thing, to actually
sew it together and bring it to a crescendo,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:53.580
and yeah, take people off the board, finish
their arcs.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.280
If Tony made it out the other side, and Iron
Man 4 was waiting there, you'd be like, [shakes

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.520
head] One too many."

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.440
" … That's the hero gig: Part of the journey
is the end."

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:07.220
It just serves to illustrate the extent to
which Marvel has its finger on the pulse of

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:11.420
its audience; it knows them well enough to
not only know what they want, but what they

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.820
might not have even known they wanted.

00:02:13.529 --> 00:02:17.760
It's true - having Stark defeat Thanos only
to pay a minimal price would have cheapened

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:18.420
his action.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:22.680
To then bring him back for Iron Man 4 would
have been a transparent cash grab of the sort

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.640
that the studio has thus far steered way clear
of.

00:02:25.989 --> 00:02:30.560
It's not even that Marvel is opposed to four-quels
for individual Avengers: Thor, after all,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.720
will be getting a fourth solo flick with the
upcoming Thor: Love and Thunder, but he is

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.280
a character who happens to be smack dab in
the midst of a huge, profound shift in his

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:39.780
priorities.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:45.900
His transformation from often-stoic, sometimes
unintentionally funny God of Thunder to hilarious,

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.999
clinically depressed, still badass Asguardian
of the Galaxy didn't begin in earnest until

00:02:50.999 --> 00:02:56.959
2017's Thor: Ragnarok - and the Earth-shattering
events of Avengers: Infinity War and Endgame

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:01.540
left him with a lot of unanswered questions
about who he really is, and also with a lot

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.400
of room for growth.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:07.960
Stark, on the other hand, underwent a complete
and convincing character arc between inaugural

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.060
MCU effort Iron Man and Endgame.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.180
The so-called "genius billionaire playboy
philanthropist" didn't even begin to learn

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:19.140
the meaning of teamwork and sacrifice until
2012's The Avengers, and the events of Iron

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.760
Man 3 and Captain America: Civil War forced
him to confront his own shortcomings in a

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.660
way that he had never before done.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.899
If Infinity War finally made him come to terms
with the possibility - heck, the cold reality

00:03:30.899 --> 00:03:35.680
- of abject failure, then Endgame saw him
learn that even such failure doesn't give

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.629
one license to simply give up - and that some
things, like the love of friends and family,

00:03:40.629 --> 00:03:42.380
are worth giving everything for.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:46.840
It was as complete and moving an arc as any
superhero could have ever hoped for.

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:51.680
Of course, this probably doesn't mean that
Marvel fans will never see Iron Man again.

00:03:51.689 --> 00:03:56.370
Signs are pointing to at least a cameo in
the upcoming solo vehicle Black Widow - which

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.979
takes place between the events of Civil War
and Infinity War - and there are conflicting

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.420
reports as to whether Downey will reprise
the role in the Disney+ animated series What

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:05.920
If...?

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:11.080
But as far as Stark's actual, complete story
in the MCU goes, we're of the opinion that

00:04:11.089 --> 00:04:15.409
it couldn't have been executed any better,
and that Markus and McFeely are absolutely

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:17.980
correct - Iron Man 4 would have been one too
many.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.450
However, we can't help but hope that Marvel
finds a way to let Taika Waititi keep on making

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:23.520
Thor movies forever.

